{
  "image_name": "ebe8d723888c4023b779ce52e15139fd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the marine nitrogen cycle across the atmosphere, ocean, sediment, and rock record, while emphasizing changes in nitrogen oxidation state and isotope fractionation. It shows nitrogen moving among major forms such as ammonium, nitrite, nitrate, dissolved N₂, nitrous oxide, and organic nitrogen through linked biological and geochemical processes.\n\nIn the ocean, nitrate is produced by nitrification and consumed by assimilation into organic matter and by dissimilatory nitrate reduction. Denitrification and anammox return fixed nitrogen to N₂, with nitrous oxide appearing as an intermediate or byproduct. Nitrogen fixation converts atmospheric N₂ into ammonium, which can then be assimilated by organisms. Organic nitrogen is recycled back to ammonium by ammonification, and some organic matter is buried by sedimentation.\n\nAt the air–sea boundary, the diagram highlights gas exchange and strong isotopic effects during ammonia volatilization. In sediments, ammonium can be adsorbed onto clays, released again during mineralization, or ultimately incorporated into the rock record through metamorphic pathways, forming nitrogen in clays, micas, and kerogens. Overall, the graphic connects redox transformations, biological cycling, burial, and isotopic signatures within one integrated nitrogen system.",
